MCP PERSISTENT TERMINAL - STATE-AWARE OPERATION

USE FOR: vi, edit, fd, reversi, watch, interactive python and
other full-screen or keyboard-driven programs. Do not use
execute_command for these programs.

SESSION
  mcpd creates one PTY and an interactive sh at startup.
  terminal_read({})       inspect current 80x24 ANSI screen
  terminal_send({text:...}) send ordinary characters (NO Enter)
  terminal_send({key:'ENTER'}) submit shell command / newline
  terminal_send({key:'ESC'})   leave vi INSERT mode
  terminal_send({key:'CTRL_C'}) interrupt foreground program
  No terminal_open or terminal_close tools exist.
  The session is shared by all MCP clients: do not assume an idle sh.

STATE CHECK BEFORE INPUT
  1. Call terminal_read({}); identify prompt, current application,
     active mode and whether it is waiting for a key or command.
  2. If a shell prompt is visible, send command text, then ENTER.
     If an app is running, use its documented keys instead.
  3. Inspect terminal_read({}) after each meaningful action.
     terminal_send reports accepted bytes, NOT a completed action.
  4. If rendering or COM move is still in progress, read again
     after the operation has had time to advance; do not resend.
  5. After quitting an app, confirm the shell prompt before typing
     another command. Never type a shell command inside an app.

KEYS VS TEXT
  Named key parameter: UP DOWN LEFT RIGHT ENTER ESC ESCAPE TAB
  SPACE SP BACKSPACE BS DELETE CTRL_A ... CTRL_Z.
  text:"L" sends an uppercase L; key:"LEFT" sends left arrow.
  text:" " or key:"SPACE" sends a space.
  A letter, including q, h, j, k, l, i, is ordinary text.
  Use key for control keys, text for literal characters.

SCREEN INTERPRETATION
  terminal_read returns the current 80x24 screen as ANSI text.
  ESC[4m/ESC[24m underline the visible cursor cell; ESC[7m/
  ESC[0m indicate reverse video. A blank cursor cell is marked.
  No separate row/column metadata, scrollback, or atomic snapshot.
  Screen may show an intermediate draw. Cursor != selection/turn.
  Read /man/pty for device and display details.

ERRORS AND RECOVERY
  The shell may restart after exit. A terminal_send detecting that
  restart RETURNS ERROR without sending the input: read screen,
  confirm the new prompt, then decide whether to send input.
  An interrupted HTTP reply or 'PTY send failed' may mean input
  was partly delivered. Read the screen; verify effects before
  retrying. NEVER blindly repeat writes or Enter.
  Avoid exiting the shared shell, killing mcpd, or starting mcpd
  inside its own PTY. No authentication or TLS: isolated LAN only.

APPLICATION TOPICS
  /man/vi       vi mode, save, quit, literal replacement
  /man/fd       viewer vs list, keys, q behavior
  /man/reversi  player turn, COM delay, legal/illegal moves
  /man/pty      PTY device details, cursor markers

EXAMPLE: READ FIRST, THEN START REVERSI
  terminal_read({})
  get_manual({topic:'reversi'})
  terminal_send({text:'reversi'})      # only at sh prompt
  terminal_send({key:'ENTER'})
  terminal_read({})
  ... send keys only when Turn: PLAYER; inspect after each move
  terminal_send({text:'q'})            # when game accepts keys
  terminal_read({})                    # confirm sh prompt
